sue 2025.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAdvanced search | BacDive (https://bacdive.dsmz.de/advsearch?pfc=1)\nciteturn52search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago;   * [Input] Acetobacterium sp. designation_header: KoMAc1 strain number: DSM 5193\n  * [Input] Acetobacterium malicum subsp. dehalogenans designation_header: MC strain number: DSM 11527, NBRC 117038\n  * [Input] Acetobacterium malicum subsp. malicum designation_header: MuME 1, MuME1 strain number: DSM 4132, ATCC 51201 type strain: Image: Yes\n  * [Input] Acetobacterium paludosum designation_header: Z-4092, Z-B12 strain number: DSM 8237 type strain: Image: Yes\n  * [Input] Acetobacterium sp. strain number: DSM 2396, Gra EG 12\n  * [Input] Acetobacterium sp. designation_header: KoMAc1 strain number: DSM 5193\n  * [Input] Acetobacterium sp. designation_header: LuTria 3 strain number: DSM 8909\n  * [Input] Acetobacterium sp. designation_header: LuPhet 1 strain number: DSM 9077\n  * [Input] Acetobacterium tundrae designation_header: Z-4493 strain number: DSM 9173 type strain: Image: Yes\n  * [Input] Acetobacterium tundrae strain number: DSM 917, NRRL B-3652, UC 3564\n  * [Input] Acetobacterium wieringae designation_header: C strain number: DSM 1911, ATCC 43740, JCM 2380 type strain: Image: Yes\n  * [Input] Acetobacterium woodii strain number: DSM 1030, ATCC 29683, JCM 2381, WB 1, NBRC 117002 type strain: Image: Yes\n  * [Input] Acetobacteroides hydrogenigenes designation_header: RL-C strain number: DSM 24657, CGMCC 1.5173, JCM 17603 type strain: Image: Yes\n  * [Input] Acetofilamentum rigidum designation_header: MN strain number: DSM 20769 type strain: Image: Yes\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFermentation of methoxyacetate to glycolate and acetate by newly isolated strains of Acetobacterium sp. (https://d-nb.info/1106331354/34)\nciteturn52search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 19.6 years ago; bacteria could be transferred over 16 subcultures with culti- ... ment, isolation and cultivation contained 30 mM sodium ... Fatty acids and methoxyacetate were determined by direct\n201 \nMedia and growth conditions \ncultures were finally obtained with the enrichment cultures \nfrom two sewage plants (strains KoMacl and G6Macl) \nand from Rio Matin, Venice (strain RMMacl). Purity was \nchecked by microscopical examination after growth in mini- \nmal medium and in complex medium (AC- medium, Difco, \nAnn Arbor, Michigan, USA). \nSimilar enrichment cultures with dimethoxyethane (eth- \nylene glycol dimethyl ether) exhibited gas production only \nin one case (sediment from a polluted freshwater creek) after \n5 months of incubation. The dimethoxyethane-degrading \nbacteria could be transferred over 16 subcultures with culti- \nvation times of 4- 6 weeks every time. Pure cultures could \nnot be isolated. In the enrichment cultures, complete conver- \nsion to CH4 and CO/with intermediate formation of acetate \ncould be demonstrated (data not shown). \nAll procedures for cultivation and isolation as well as all \nmethods for analysis of metabolic products were essentially \nas described in earlier papers (Widdel and Pfennig 1981; \nSchink and Pfennig 1982). The mineral medium for enrich- \nment, isolation and cultivation contained 30 mM sodium \nbicarbonate as buffer, 1 mM sodium sulfide as reducing \nagent, the trace element solution SL 10 (Widdel et at. 1983) \nand a vitamin solution (Pfennig 1978). Freshwater medium \ncontained 0.5 g NaC1 and 0.4 g MgCl2 x 6 HzO , saltwater \nmedium 20.0 g and 3.0 g, respectively, per liter. The pH was \n7.1 - 7.3. Growth experiments were carried out at 30 ~ C. For \nisolation of pure cultures, the agar shake dilution method \nwas applied (Pfennig 1978). Gram staining was carried out \nafter Magee et al. (1975); the Gram type was also checked \nby the KOH method (Gregersen 1978). \nCytological properties of pure cultures \nChemical determinations and chemicals \nSulfide was determined after Cline (1969), nitrite after Pro- \nchhzkov~i (1959), protein after Zamenhoff (1957), carbon \nmonoxide dehydrogenase after Diekert and Thauer (1978). \nFatty acids and methoxyacetate were determined by direct \ngas chromatography on a packed column (Schink and Pfen- \nnig 1982), glycolic acid on a glas capillary column after \nmethylation (Dehning and Schink 1989). \nAll chemicals used were of analytical or reagent grade \nquality and were obtained from Merck, Darmstadt, Sigma, \nMiinchen, and Fluka, Neu-Ulm, FRG. \nResults \nEnrichment and isolation \nThe two freshwater strains KoMacl and GrMacl were mo- \ntile rods, 0.9-1.0 • 2.0- 4.0 ~tm in size, with slightly pointed \nends (Fig. 1 a, b). In older cultures, they tended to form \nchains of several cells which were sometimes swollen in the \nmiddle. Both strains stained Gram-positive; results of the \nKOH test indicated a Gram-positive cell wall architecture \nas well. Cells of strain RMMacl were immotile, rather small \nrods, 0.5-0.7• \nin size, with rounded ends \n(Fig. 1 c). They stained Gram-negative, but both the KOH \ntest as well as electron microscopic examination indicated a \nGram-positive cell wall architecture (Fig. 2). In Indian ink \npreparations, thin slime capsules became visible. No strain \nformed spores, neither in defined medium nor in a special \nsporulation medium (Hollaus and Sleytr 1972). Pasteurized \ncultures did not show growth after more than 6 weeks \nof incubation. The guanine-plus-cytosine content of the \nDNA \nwas \n42.7_+ 1.0mo1% \nwith \nstrain \nKoMacl, \n44.4 -t- 1.0 mol% with strain GrMacl, and 47.8 _+ 1.0 tool% \nwith strain RMMacl. No cytochromes could be detected by \nredox difference spectroscopy of crude cell extracts of either \nstrain. \nPhysiology \nEnrichment cultures with freshwater or saltwater medium \ncontaining 10 mM methoxyacetate were inoculated with \nabout 5 ml sediment and sludge samples from various \nsources, including marine and freshwater sediments, as well \nas sewage sludge from two municipal sewage plants. Gas \nproduction started in all cases after 3 - 5 weeks, and subcul- \ntures were subsequently inoculated with 5 ml culture fluid, \nusually after 2-3 weeks of incubation. Pure cultures were \nisolated from these cultures by two subsequent agar shake \ndilution series with methoxyacetate as substrate. \nFreshwater cultures formed yellowish lens-shaped colo- \nnies, marine cultures small white colonies in the agar. Pure \nThe strains KoMacl and RMMacl grew well in mineral \nmedium containing NaC1 from 0.05 up to 2.0% and \nMgC12 x 6 H20 from 0.04 to 0.3 % (w/v), strain G6Macl \ngrew in medium with 0.05-1.5% NaC1 and 0.04-0.4% \nMgC12 x 6 H20. Phosphate inhibited growth of strains \nFig. la-c \nPhase-contrast photomicrographs \nof cells of the new isolates, a Strain \nKoMacl, b G6Macl, c strain \nRMMacl. Bar equals 10 gm for all \nthree pictures--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBioresource Technology 427 (2025) 131913 (https://edepot.wur.nl/690046)\nciteturn52search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; KoMAc1\nDSM 5193\n1977;\nCP002987\nno\n4,044,777\n39.3\nPoehlein et al.,\n2012\nBache and\nPfennig, 1981\nAcetobacterium sp.\nGra EG 12\nDSM 2396\nH2 + CO2\nacetate\nn.r.\nn.r.\nSchink and Stieb,\n1983\nno genome sequence\nAcetobacterium sp.\nKoMAc1\nDSM 5193\nH2 + CO2\nacetate\n30\n6.8-7.0\nSchuppert and\nSchink, 1990\nno genome sequence\nAcetobacterium sp.\nLuPhet1\nDSM 9077\nH2 + CO2\nacetate\n25-30\n7.0-7.5\nFrings and\nSchink, 1994\nno genome sequence\nAcetobacterium sp.\nLuTria3\nDSM 8909\nH2 + CO2\nacetate\n25-30\n7.1-7.4\nFrings et al.,\n1994\nno genome sequence\n5 \nEubacterium aggregans\nSR12\nDSM 12183T\nH2 + CO2\nacetate,\nformate\n35\n7.2\nMechichi et al.,\n1998\nFNRK00000000\nn.d.\n2,833,471\n48.6\nn.p.\n37\n7.0\nMountfort et al.,\n1988;\nJAVVAB000000000\nn.d.\n4,369,831\n47.0\nFlaiz et al., 2024\nEubacterium callanderi\nFD\nDSM 3662T\nMeOH\nacetate,\nbutyrate,\nhexanoat\nFlaiz et al., 2024\n37-40\n7.5\nLynd et al., 1982;\nCP132155-CP132156\nyes\n4,278,751\n47.5\nFlaiz et al., 2024\nZeikus et al.,\n1980;\nEubacterium callanderi\n(formerly\nButyribacterium\nmethylotrophicum)\nMarburg\nDSM 3468\nH2 + CO2,\nCO (after\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHandbook of (https://www.saetw.org/ebooks/biomicro/236.pdf)\nciteturn52search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.9 years ago; Acetobacterium sp. ... dition of 10–20mg sodium dithionite per liter from a 5% (w/v) solu- ... Methoxyacetate.............................................................................0.9g\nTrace elements solution ...........................................................10.0mL\nVitamin solution.......................................................................10.0mL\nNaHCO3 solution.....................................................................10.0mL\nSubstrate solution.....................................................................10.0mL\nNa2S·9H2O solution.................................................................10.0mL\npH 7.5 ± 0.2 at 25°C\nSolution C:\nComposition per 100.0mL:\nNaHCO3........................................................................................5.0g\nSubstrate Solution:\nComposition per 10.0mL:\nNa-lactate...................................................................................... 4.0g\nPreparation of Solution C: Add NaHCO3 to distilled/deionized\nwater and bring volume to 100.0mL Mix thoroughly. Filter sterilize.\nFlush with 80% N2  + 20% CO2 to remove dissolved oxygen. \nSolution D:\nComposition per 10.0mL:\nMethoxyacetate.............................................................................0.9g\nPreparation of Substrate Solution: Add Na-lactate to distilled/\ndeionized water and bring volume to 10.0mL. Mix thoroughly. Sparge\nwith 10% N2. Filter sterilize.\nPreparation of Solution D: Add methoxyacetate to distilled/deion-\nized water and bring volume to 10.0mL. Mix thoroughly. Sparge with\n100% N2. Autoclave for 15 min at 15 psi pressure–121°C.\nNaHCO3 Solution:\nComposition per 10.0mL:\nNaHCO3........................................................................................ 1.0g\n© 2010 by Taylor and Francis Group, LLC--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSpecific mechanism of Acidithiobacillus caldus extracellular polymeric substances in the bioleaching of copper-bearing sulfide ore (https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article/file?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0213945&type=printable)\nciteturn52search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.6 years ago; Crawled: 7.6 years ago; In the AD system, the granular sulfur on the ore surface could not be used by attached cells and therefore formed the S⁰ passivation layer, inhibiting subsequent sulfur oxidation. ... Additionally, the final values of Eh in deficient systems (485–501 mV) with an inactive iron/sulfur ion level were slightly lower than those in the BC system (523 mV). The bioleaching efficiency of chalcopyrite by thermophiles (*Acidianus brierleyi, Sulfolobus metallicus* and *Metallosphaera sedula*) is closely associated with the redox status fluctuation [31].\nAdditionally, the final values of Eh in deficient systems (485–501 mV) with an inactive iron/sulfur ion level were slightly lower than those in the BC system (523 mV). The bioleaching efficiency of chalcopyrite by thermophiles (*Acidianus brierleyi, Sulfolobus metallicus* and *Metallosphaera sedula*) is closely associated with the redox status fluctuation [31].\n\nThe sulfur metabolism and iron metabolism interacted with each other, as shown in Eqs (1)–(4). The highest concentrations of ferrous ions in each system (in g/L) were 1.20 (BC), 0.35 (PD), 0.30 (AD) and 0.27 (ED). It has been reported that the adsorption behavior of attached cells in the early stage is beneficial to further concentrate ferric ions and attack sulfide ore, as shown in Eq (1) [20]. The concentration of ferrous ions showed the greatest reduction, reaching 77.5%, in the attached cells /EPS-deficient system. Similarly, the sulfur metabolism of another mesophilic sulfur-oxidizer, *A. thiooxidans*, was more significantly influenced by attached cells, compared to the iron/sulfur-oxidizer, *A. ferrooxidans* [27]. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSpecific mechanism of Acidithiobacillus caldus extracellular polymeric substances in the bioleaching of copper-bearing sulfide ore (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6461249/)\nciteturn52search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.6 years ago; Crawled: last month; The main biochemical reactions in the bioleaching of copper-bearing sulfide ore are summarized in Equations (Eq) ([ref-type: disp-formula] 1 [Source reference: [Source link 24]])–([ref-type: disp-formula] 6 [Source reference: [Source link 25]]). ... Additionally, the final values of Eh in deficient systems (485–501 mV) with an inactive iron/sulfur ion level were slightly lower than those in the BC system (523 mV). The bioleaching efficiency of chalcopyrite by thermophiles (Acidianus brierleyi, Sulfolobus metallicus and Metallosphaera sedula) is closely associated with the redox status fluctuation [[ref-type: bibr] 31 [Source reference: [Source link 29]]].\nAdditionally, the final values of Eh in deficient systems (485–501 mV) with an inactive iron/sulfur ion level were slightly lower than those in the BC system (523 mV). The bioleaching efficiency of chalcopyrite by thermophiles (Acidianus brierleyi, Sulfolobus metallicus and Metallosphaera sedula) is closely associated with the redox status fluctuation [[ref-type: bibr] 31 [Source reference: [Source link 29]]].\n\n* * *\n\nIdentifier: [pub-id-type: doi] 10.1371/journal.pone.0213945.g002 Fig 2\n\nCaption:\n\n#### Changes in key chemical parameters in different deficient bioleaching systems.\n\n(A): pH; (B): Eh; (C): Sulfate ions; (D): Ferrous ions; (E): Ferric ions; (F) Planktonic and attached biomass; (G) μ_{x} of planktonic and attached cells. Note: the pH of leachate during bioleaching was measured before the pH-1.5 adjustment by 6 M HCl every two days.\n\n[Source locator: pone.0213945.g002.jpg]\n\n* * *\n\nThe sulfur metabolism and iron metabolism interacted with each other, as shown in Eqs ([ref-type: disp-formula] 1 [Source reference: [Source link 30]])–([ref-type: disp-formula] 4 [Source reference: [Source link 31]]). --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDifference spectra of membrane preparations of Sulfobacillus sibiricus... | Download Scientific Diagram (https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Difference-spectra-of-membrane-preparations-of-Sulfobacillus-sibiricus-N1T-from-the_fig2_47510590)\nciteturn52search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 16.2 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; Metallosphaera and Sulfolobus, representing the Crenarchaeota, both yielded spectra dominated by the same novel cytochrome as that observed in the Nitrospirae and a new, heretofore unrecognized redox-active prosthetic group with a reduced peak at around 485 nm.\nAcidihalobacter, also representing the Proteobacteria, yielded a spectrum in which both cytochrome b and a long-wavelength cytochrome a were clearly visible. Two species of Leptospirillum, representing the Nitrospirae, both yielded spectra that were dominated by a cytochrome with a reduced peak at 579 nm. Sulfobacillus and Alicyclobacillus, representing the Gram-positive Firmicutes, both yielded spectra dominated by a-type cytochromes. Acidimicrobium and Ferrimicrobium, representing the Gram-positive Actinobacteria, also yielded spectra dominated by a-type cytochromes. Acidiplasma and Ferroplasma, representing the Euryarchaeota, both yielded spectra dominated by a ba3-type of cytochrome. Metallosphaera and Sulfolobus, representing the Crenarchaeota, both yielded spectra dominated by the same novel cytochrome as that observed in the Nitrospirae and a new, heretofore unrecognized redox-active prosthetic group with a reduced peak at around 485 nm. These observations are consistent with the hypothesis that individual acidophilic microorganisms that respire aerobically on iron utilize one of at least six different types of electron transfer pathways that are characterized by different redox-active prosthetic groups. In situ absorbance spectroscopy is shown to be a useful complement to existing means of investigating the details of energy conservation in intact microorganisms under physiological conditions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Genome Sequence of the Metal-Mobilizing, Extremely Thermoacidophilic Archaeon Metallosphaera sedula Provides Insights into Bioleaching-Associated Metabolism (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2227735/)\nciteturn52search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 18.9 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; However, the complete genome sequence of the extremely thermoacidophilic archaeon Metallosphaera sedula DSM 5348 (2.2 Mb, ∼2,300 open reading frames [ORFs]) provides insights into biologically catalyzed metal sulfide oxidation. ... Extreme thermophiles are particularly desirable, as problematic passivation on ore surfaces decreases at higher temperatures.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAcetobacterium (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/316061467_Acetobacterium)\nciteturn52search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; Fermentation of methoxyacetate to glycolate and acetate by newly isolated strains of Acetobacterium sp ... Three strains of new mesophilic homoacetogenic bacteria were enriched and isolated from sewage sludge and from marine sediment samples with methoxyacetate as sole organic substrate in a carbonate-buffered medium under anoxic conditions. ... Presence of a sodium‐transl